{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_0145WYCwDdtJn1KaKzcUeXD4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me start implementing the changes systematically.\n\nLet me first create the KEY_DECISIONS.md file, then implement the engine changes (E1, E2, E3), then implement the cards.\n\n### Plan:\n\n**E1**: In `engine/casting.py`, after `game.stack.push(stack_obj)`, fire `SpellCastTriggeredEvent`.\n\n**E2**: In `engine/events.py`, add `BeginningOfPrecombatMainTriggeredEvent`. In `engine/game_state.py`, fire it in `advance_phase` after `empty_mana_pools()` when the new phase is `PRECOMBAT_MAIN`.\n**E3**: I need to extend `get_cost_reduction` in `engine/casting.py` to check each permanent on the controller's battlefield for a `spell_cost_reduction` method and apply any reductions it provides.\n\nThen I'll implement the card effects in order: sos_4, sos_1, sos_245, sos_226, sos_257, sos_57, sos_120, sos_13, sos_201, sos_97. For sos_257 specifically, I need to add a lightweight restriction marker to mana in the pool to handle the restricted mana approach.\n\nLooking at how ManaPool works with its simple dict structure, I'll add a separate `_restricted_pool` dictionary to track mana that can only be spent on specific spell types. When paying for a spell, I'll check if the remaining mana includes restricted mana and only allow it to satisfy the cost if the restriction matches—so restricted mana from sos_257 can only pay for instants and sorceries.\n\nThe challenge is that `can_pay` and `pay` don't currently know what spell is being cast, so I need to trace through the casting flow to see where this information is available. Looking at `engine/casting.py`'s `cast_spell` function, it handles cost reduction before checking if the player can pay, so I'll need to pass the spell type information down to the payment methods. I could either temporarily reduce the mana pool during the payment check for non-instant/sorcery spells, or track restricted mana separately and only use it when casting instants or sorceries. The TODO suggests adding a lightweight restriction marker to the mana pool itself, so I'll add a `_restricted_count` field and check it during payment—though the challenge is that the payment logic doesn't know the card type, so I need to pass that context through or handle it at the casting level. I could add an `add_restricted` method to track restricted mana separately, then modify `can_pay` to accept an `allow_restricted` flag that determines whether restricted mana counts toward payment—instant/sorcery spells would pass `True`, while other spells would pass `False`. The casting code would then check this flag when validating whether the player can afford the spell. Adding restricted mana tracking... The cleanest solution is to keep restricted mana completely separate in its own dictionary, then add a parallel set of payment methods that include restricted mana when needed. For instant and sorcery spells, I'll use these inclusive versions during casting, while regular spells only check the normal pool. I'm adding the restricted mana tracking to the ManaPool class with methods to add and clear it, plus a new payment check that combines both pools. I'm setting up the mana pool structure with separate tracking for restricted mana that can only be used for instant/sorcery spells, along with a method to add restricted mana and an empty function to clear the pools.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the casting logic — when casting an instant or sorcery, I need to temporarily merge the restricted mana into the available pool for payment checks, then clean it up afterward. But I'm realizing this gets complicated since `cast_spell_free` doesn't involve mana payment at all, so the restriction only really matters for regular spell casting. Let me reconsider the spec: the restricted mana should only be spendable for instant or sorcery spells, so I need to handle that constraint during the payment phase. Adding helper methods... Modifying `can_pay` and `pay` with an optional `for_instant_sorcery` parameter is additive and won't break existing calls since it defaults to `False`. I'll add the restricted mana tracking to `mana.py` with a new `_restricted` dict, update `empty()` to clear it, and add methods to manage restricted mana while updating `can_pay` to check both pools when the flag is set.\n\nIn `casting.py`, I'll detect instant and sorcery spells after calculating effective cost, then pass `for_instant_sorcery=True` to the payment methods. The tricky part is merging the restricted and regular pools temporarily during payment calculation while tracking which colors come from each source.\n\nI'm settling on a simpler approach: when `for_instant_sorcery=True`, treat the available pool as the sum of both restricted and regular mana, but drain from the regular pool first before touching the restricted pool. This keeps the logic straightforward for both `can_pay` and `pay` methods. , 1)` to add restricted mana, then check if the mana pool can pay for a spell's cost using `can_pay(cost, for_instant_sorcery=True)` when casting instants or sorceries, or just `can_pay(cost)` for other spells to ensure only unrestricted mana is used. The casting pipeline needs to detect whether a card is an instant or sorcery and pass that flag when checking and paying mana costs.\n\nNow I'm implementing the actual `can_pay` and `pay` methods in the mana pool class — when `for_instant_sorcery=True`, I'll combine the unrestricted and restricted pools together, then drain from unrestricted first before touching restricted mana. Finishing the reconciliation logic...\n\nNow I'm ready to move forward with implementation. I'll document the key decisions first, then work through the engine changes systematically, testing as I go.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":