{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_016uyc4iatMp2MWtZwZ1oQmw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The delayed trigger isn't firing when advancing to the next main phase. The issue is likely that `advance_to_phase` directly sets the game phase without going through `advance_phase`, so the `BeginningOfPrecombatMainTriggeredEvent` doesn't fire for the trigger we registered.\n\nActually, `advance_to_phase` calls `game.advance_phase()` repeatedly. And I put the event firing in `advance_phase` when the new phase is PRECOMBAT_MAIN. So the event SHOULD fire.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the event fires inside `advance_phase`, which puts a trigger on the stack. \n\nThe real problem might be the condition check: `g.turn_number > cast_turn` won't be satisfied if we're still in the same turn when advancing phases. Let me trace through the scenario to see what the turn number actually is when the trigger evaluates. I'm clearing the mana pools and resetting the active player back to player 1, then advancing to the precombat main phase. Since we're likely already in that phase after the sculpt spell resolves, the advance call will return immediately without making any changes. If we're already in PRECOMBAT_MAIN, advancing to that phase returns immediately without triggering the event, so the turn number stays the same. This means the condition `g.turn_number > cast_turn` depends on whether we've actually moved to a new turn or not. Looking at the test setup, p2 starts their turn in PRECOMBAT_MAIN, casts target, then the board state changes—but I need to verify we're setting up the right phase for p1 to cast sculpt.\n\nSince ManaSculpt is an Instant, it can be cast at instant speed, so the timing check passes. After casting and resolving, we're still in whatever phase we were in with the same turn number, then the test empties mana pools and switches the active player back to p1 before advancing to PRECOMBAT_MAIN again.\n\nBut if we're already at PRECOMBAT_MAIN, advancing to it again is a no-op and won't trigger the event. I need to advance through the rest of the current turn first, cycling through cleanup and into the next turn's PRECOMBAT_MAIN, so the turn number actually increments and the event fires properly. Adding a flag-based approach...\n\nThe key insight is that `BeginningOfPrecombatMainTriggeredEvent` fires when entering PRECOMBAT_MAIN, so if we register the trigger while already in that phase, it won't fire again until we cycle through the phases and return. I need to trace through the exact sequence: if we're in p2's PRECOMBAT_MAIN when sculpt resolves, we'd need to advance through combat and postcombat to reach p1's next PRECOMBAT_MAIN.\n\nThe real problem with the test is that after sculpt resolves, calling `advance_to_phase(game, Phase.PRECOMBAT_MAIN)` when we're already there does nothing. A cleaner fix would be to use a simple boolean flag that allows the trigger to fire once on any main phase for the controller, rather than relying on turn number comparisons. The spec says \"at the beginning of your next main phase\"—if the spell resolves during your current main phase, the next one is either later this turn or next turn depending on the game state. I could use a flag on the sentinel object itself to track whether the trigger has already fired, which would be cleaner than relying on turn numbers. The real issue is that advancing phases in the test is tricky—going from PRECOMBAT_MAIN to the next turn's PRECOMBAT_MAIN requires moving through the entire turn sequence, and I need to be careful about the phase ordering. The trigger was registered after the initial PRECOMBAT_MAIN event fired during setup, so it won't trigger until the next turn's PRECOMBAT_MAIN phase. I'm tracing through the test sequence to see where the event should fire and why it's not being caught.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"