{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_012uN7iRNkvCougPF9pxvyNX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The mana.py looks fine. Let me check `ManaCost.parse(\"{W}\")` to understand if there's an issue with pips.\n\nLet me look at `can_pay` - when `for_instant_sorcery=True`:\n```python\nremaining: dict[ManaType, int] = {\n    mt: self._pool.get(mt, 0) + self._restricted.get(mt, 0)\n    for mt in ManaType\n}\n```\n\nThen:\n```python\nfor mana_type, count in cost.pips.items():\n    if remaining.get(mana_type, 0) < count:\n        return False\n    remaining[mana_type] = remaining.get(mana_type, 0) - count\n```\n\nSo `{W}` should have `pips = {ManaType.WHITE: 1}`, and with `remaining[ManaType.WHITE] = 1`, it should work. But I'm noticing line 105 has a duplicate `remaining: dict[ManaType, int]` annotation in the else branch - in Python 3.10+, you can't have two type annotations for the same variable in the same scope, which could be causing a syntax or runtime issue.\n\nActually, wait - Python does allow duplicate annotations at runtime since they're just metadata stored in `__annotations__`. The variable assignment itself happens normally, so this shouldn't cause an error, though strict linters might complain about it.\n\nLet me reconsider what's actually happening here. Local variable annotations in function bodies aren't even stored anywhere at runtime - they're essentially ignored. So the real issue with the mana must be something else entirely. Looking back at the actual error message about insufficient mana for 'Swords to Plowshares', I need to trace through the test setup more carefully.\n\nThe problem is that `advance_to_phase` calls `empty_mana_pools()`, which clears all mana before the spell can be cast. I need to add the mana after advancing to the phase, not before. For the other test failure with the prepared ability, I should also revisit the logic around how the inkling gets assigned to 